{
  "abstract": "The ambidextrous moving sofa problem asks for the largest area of a connected planar shape that can be moved, from one starting position, around the right-angled corner of a hallway of unit width that turns right, and also around the corner of a hallway that turns left. Dan Romik (Experimental Mathematics, 2018) constructed a shape of area M = 1 + 4Y^2 + arctan Y = 1.64495..., where Y = 0.2980... is the positive root of 4Y^3 + 3Y = 1, and proposed it as a likely solution; in 2025 Georgiev, Gómez-Serrano, Tao and Wagner listed the problem as open.\n\nThis entry proves that Romik's ambidextrous sofa is optimal and that it is the only optimal shape. Every compact connected set that continuous rigid motions, starting in the same orientation, move through both hallways has area at most M, with equality if and only if the set is the image of Romik's sofa by a rotation and a translation. It also proves that Romik's sofa and its images by rotations and translations move through both hallways, that its area is M, that its convex hull is the convex body given by its support function, and that M is Romik's closed form (3 + 2 sqrt 2)^(1/3) + (3 - 2 sqrt 2)^(1/3) - 1 + arctan(((sqrt 2 + 1)^(1/3) - (sqrt 2 - 1)^(1/3))/2).\n\nThe proof splits the convex hull of a sofa into two caps, one for each hallway, and bounds the area of the sofa by the sum of their scores. The score of each cap is at most M/2, with equality only for Romik's cap, and when the area is M the sofa is recovered from its caps. Two steps use Jineon Baek's theorem that Gerver's sofa is optimal for the ordinary moving sofa problem (arXiv:2411.19826); its Lean formalization comes from lean4-moving-sofa and is included in the repository.",
